/*
==========================
      C++ VECTOR NOTES
==========================

1. EMPTY VECTOR
----------------
std::vector<int> vec;

- size = 0
- no elements exist
- vec[0] is INVALID (crash / undefined behavior)

Add elements using:
vec.push_back(value);

Example:
vec.push_back(10);
vec.push_back(20);
// vec = [10, 20]


2. ACCESSING ELEMENTS
----------------------
vec[0] -> first element
vec[1] -> second element

⚠ Only works if elements exist!


3. VECTOR WITH SIZE
-------------------
std::vector<int> vec(3);

- creates 3 elements
- all initialized to 0 (for int)

Example:
vec = [0, 0, 0]

You can still expand it:
vec.push_back(5);
// vec = [0, 0, 0, 5]


4. VECTOR WITH VALUES
---------------------
std::vector<int> vec = {1, 2, 3};

or

std::vector<int> vec{1, 2, 3};

Result:
vec = [1, 2, 3]


5. IMPORTANT IDEA
-----------------
- vector size = current number of elements
- NOT a fixed limit
- grows automatically with push_back()


6. COMMON MISTAKE
------------------
std::vector<int> vec;
vec[0] = 10;   // ❌ WRONG (no memory allocated yet)

Correct:
vec.push_back(10);  // ✅


7. SUMMARY
----------
- empty vector → use push_back()
- sized vector → pre-filled with 0s
- initializer list → set values directly
- vectors always grow dynamically

To remove an element from a specific index :

vec.erase(vec.begin() + 1); vec.begin() points to index 0, so vec.begin() + 1 is the second element(index 1)

*/